use std
into st_cut.amni

Amni-Stitch cut files. It turns the pattern pieces from st_geom into cutting-machine geometry in millimetres (y down, like the pattern, whose own lengths are centimetres, so multiply by ten), nests them on sheets, and writes SVG, DXF and HPGL files. A piece's stitching line is `PTS`, its seam-allowance cutting line is `OFF`, an edge's allowance is `SAS` (zero or less on a fold edge), its marks are `MARKS` and its grainline ends are `g0` and `g1`, all from st_geom together with `Pt`, `P`, `padd`, `psub`, `pmul`, `plen`, `pdist`, `pnorm`, `pperp` and `out_normal`. All cut geometry lives in flat pools so one primitive is a range of points. Write compact Helium: if expressions instead of if blocks wherever possible, short bodies on one line, no repeated code. Never hold a slice of a pool while pushing onto the same pool: read each point into a local by index first. `esc` from st_svg escapes text for XML.

```amni
import math;
import st_geom;
import st_body;
import st_draft;
import st_svg;
let LY_CUT = 0; let LY_SCORE = 1; let LY_ENGRAVE = 2;
let PK_PATH = 0; let PK_LOOP = 1; let PK_CIRC = 2; let PK_TEXT = 3;
let CU_CW = 2.0;
let CU_TW = 0.275;
let CU_HOLE = 1.5;
let CU_BTN = 2.0;
let CU_SLOT = 2.0;
let CU_OVER = 10.0;
let CU_NOTCH = 7.0;
let CU_NOTCH0 = 3.0;
let CU_PRESETS = 13;
let CU_NAMES const byte[:][13] = ["Letter 8.5 × 11 in (216 × 279 mm)", "Legal 8.5 × 14 in (216 × 356 mm)", "Tabloid 11 × 17 in (279 × 432 mm)", "A4 8.3 × 11.7 in (210 × 297 mm)", "A3 11.7 × 16.5 in (297 × 420 mm)", "A1 23.4 × 33.1 in (594 × 841 mm)", "A0 33.1 × 46.8 in (841 × 1189 mm)", "Cutting mat 12 × 12 in (305 × 305 mm)", "Cutting mat 12 × 24 in (305 × 610 mm)", "Sheet 24 × 36 in (610 × 914 mm)", "Roll 36 in (914 mm), any length", "Fabric 60 in (1524 mm), any length", "Custom size"];
let CU_PW const real[13] = [215.9, 215.9, 279.4, 210.0, 297.0, 594.0, 841.0, 304.8, 304.8, 609.6, 914.4, 1524.0, 0.0];
let CU_PH const real[13] = [279.4, 355.6, 431.8, 297.0, 420.0, 841.0, 1189.0, 304.8, 609.6, 914.4, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0];
struct CuSet { fmt int = 0, sheet int = 10, cw real = 304.8, ch real = 457.2, margin real = 7.0, gap real = 5.0, grain bool = true, seam bool = true, layers bool = true }
var CU = CuSet {};
struct CuPrim { kind int, layer int, first int, n int, r real, rot real, sheet int, tx int, tn int }
struct CuItem { piece int, copy int, copies int, first int, n int, cut int, w real, h real, area real, grain bool, lx real, ly real, lh real, ox real, oy real, tr int, tc int, tiled bool, sheet int, rot int, px real, py real }
struct CuSheet { w real, h real, roll bool, used real, sky int, count int }
var CUP Pt[>..];
var CUB byte[>..];
var CUR CuPrim[>..];
var CUI CuItem[>..];
var CUS CuSheet[>..];
var CUK real[>..];
var CULP Pt[>..];
var CUSP Pt[>..];
var CUNQ Pt[>..];
var CUNB Pt[>..];
var CUHQ Pt[>..];
var CUHR real[>..];
var CUC1 Pt[>..];
var CUC2 Pt[>..];
var CUTOP real[>..];
var CUBOT real[>..];
var CURT real[>..];
var CURB real[>..];
var CU_FS = -1;
var CU_FE = -1;
var CU_FA = Pt { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 };
var CU_FD = Pt { x: 1.0, y: 0.0 };
var CU_DG = 0;
var CU_TOTAL = 0.0;
var CU_USED = 0.0;
```

`cu_rot(p, r)` turns the point p by r quarter turns about the origin: r 0 gives p unchanged, r 1 gives minus y and x, r 2 gives minus x and minus y, any other r gives y and minus x.

`cu_num(out, v, dec)` appends v with dec decimals using `format_flt`; when the absolute value of v is below half a unit of the last decimal place it appends zero with dec decimals instead, so no minus zero is ever written.

`cu_sarea(pts)` is the signed area of the closed polygon whose corners are the points of the slice pts, by the shoelace sum divided by two, in the same sign convention as `piece_area`.

`cu_bbox(pts)` returns the smallest x, smallest y, largest x and largest y of the points of the slice pts, in that order.

`cu_inside(pts, p)` tells whether the point p lies inside the closed polygon pts by the even-odd ray rule, casting a ray towards positive x.

`pr_new(kind, layer, r)` appends a `CuPrim` to `CUR` with that kind, layer and r, first equal to the current length of `CUP`, n 0, rot 0, sheet minus one, tx 0 and tn 0, and returns its index. The points of a primitive are pushed to `CUP` straight after, before the next primitive is started.

`pr_pt(i, p)` pushes the point p onto `CUP` and adds one to the n of `CUR` entry i.

`pr_text(layer, at, h, rot, s)` appends a `PK_TEXT` primitive with the one point at, r equal to the text height h, rot equal to the rotation in degrees, and the characters s appended to `CUB`, with tx the start of them in `CUB` and tn their count.

`pr_line(layer, a, b)` appends a `PK_PATH` primitive of the two points a and b.

`pr_circ(layer, c, r)` appends a `PK_CIRC` primitive with the centre c as its one point and radius r.

`cu_fold(k)` finds the fold of piece k. It sets `CU_FS` and `CU_FE` to minus one and returns when the piece's fold flag is false. Otherwise, with n the piece's point count and f its first point index, it looks for the lowest s below n whose edge allowance `SAS` at f plus s is zero or less while the allowance of the previous edge, at f plus ((s plus n minus one) modulo n), is above zero. If there is none it stops. It then walks e forward from s one edge at a time, modulo n, while the allowance of edge e is zero or less, counting the steps; when the count reaches n it stops. Otherwise `CU_FS` is s and `CU_FE` is the e where the walk ended. Last, with a the stitching point `PTS` at f plus s and b the one at f plus e: when they are less than 0.0001 apart both go back to minus one, otherwise `CU_FA` is a and `CU_FD` is the unit vector from a to b.

`cu_reflect_dir(v)` mirrors the direction v across the fold axis direction `CU_FD`: twice the dot product of v and `CU_FD`, times `CU_FD`, minus v.

`cu_reflect(p)` mirrors the point p across the fold axis: `CU_FA` plus `cu_reflect_dir` of p minus `CU_FA`.

`cu_loop(k, off, out)` appends to the vector out the closed outline of piece k in centimetres, taking its n points at index f plus i from `OFF` when off is true and from `PTS` when it is false, where f is the piece's first index. When `CU_FS` is minus one it appends all n points in order. Otherwise it unfolds the piece across the fold axis: m is ((`CU_FS` minus `CU_FE` plus n) modulo n) plus one and h(j) is the point at index f plus ((`CU_FE` plus j) modulo n) for j from 0 to m minus one. When off is true, h(0) and h(m minus one) are first moved onto the axis, each replaced by `CU_FA` plus `CU_FD` times the dot product of the point minus `CU_FA` with `CU_FD`. It appends h(0) up to h(m minus one) in order, then the reflections `cu_reflect` of h(m minus two) down to h(1) in that order.

`cu_marks(k)` clears `CUNQ`, `CUNB`, `CUHQ` and `CUHR`, then walks the marks of piece k, those from its mfirst for its mn marks, still in centimetres. A `MK_NOTCH` mark pushes its a onto `CUNQ` and its b onto `CUNB`. A `MK_DART` mark pushes its b (the apex) onto `CUHQ` with radius `CU_HOLE` onto `CUHR`. A `MK_BUTTON` mark pushes its a with radius `CU_BTN`. A `MK_DOT` mark pushes its a with radius `CU_HOLE`. A `MK_DART2` mark pushes its a with radius `CU_HOLE` and, when another mark of the piece follows, also the a of that next mark with radius `CU_HOLE`, and skips that next mark. Every other kind is ignored. Then, when `CU_FS` is not minus one, it appends mirrored copies of what was collected before the mirroring started: each notch point through `cu_reflect` with its normal through `cu_reflect_dir`, skipping a notch whose point is less than 0.1 from its reflection; each hole centre through `cu_reflect` with the same radius, skipping a hole whose centre is less than 0.05 from its reflection.

`cu_slots()` cuts a slot into the closed cutting loop `CULP` for each notch of `CUNQ` and `CUNB`; both are in millimetres by now. For a notch with point a and normal b it looks at every edge of the loop (from point i to point i plus one, modulo the count, with the loop's signed area from `cu_sarea`) whose `out_normal` has a dot product with b of at least 0.3, and takes the one whose segment is nearest to a; the distance is d and the foot is the nearest point on that segment, at distance t along the edge from its first point. The notch is dropped when no edge qualifies, d is above 40 or the edge is shorter than 4. The slot depth is the smaller of `CU_NOTCH` and 0.7 times d when d is at least 2 and `CU.seam` is true, and `CU_NOTCH0` otherwise. t is limited to between 1 and the edge length minus 1. Then `CULP` is rebuilt: for each edge i in order, point i is pushed, followed by the slots of that edge in ascending t, skipping a slot closer than `CU_SLOT` to the previous slot of the same edge. A slot is four points: with u the unit vector along the edge, w the inward normal (the negative of the `out_normal`), and foot the point at distance t along the edge: foot minus u times half of `CU_SLOT`; that point plus w times the depth; foot plus u times half of `CU_SLOT` plus w times the depth; foot plus u times half of `CU_SLOT`.

`cu_grain(g0, g1)` appends the grainline of a piece, both ends already in millimetres in the item's own coordinates, as `LY_ENGRAVE` primitives: `pr_line` from g0 to g1, then for each end an arrow head as a three-point `PK_PATH` primitive: with d the unit vector pointing from the other end towards that end (the tip) and nrm the `pperp` of d, the points are the tip minus d times 8 plus nrm times 4, the tip, and the tip minus d times 8 minus nrm times 4.

`cu_prepare(k)` readies the scratch pools for piece k in millimetres: it calls `cu_fold(k)`, clears `CULP` and `CUSP`, calls `cu_loop(k, CU.seam, CULP)` and, only when `CU.seam` is true, `cu_loop(k, false, CUSP)`, calls `cu_marks(k)`, multiplies every point of `CULP`, `CUSP`, `CUNQ` and `CUHQ` by ten with `pmul`, and then calls `cu_slots()`.

`cu_label(i, tile)` appends the label text primitive of item i (an index into `CUI`) on `LY_ENGRAVE` using `pr_text`. The text is the name of the item's piece (`PIECES` entry `piece`, its name field), then when the item's copies is above one a space, copy plus one, a slash and copies, then when the tile text is not empty a space and the tile text. The label must stay inside the box of the item's own cut loop, the smallest and largest x and y of the points of its cut primitive (`CUR` at its cut). Its height starts as the item's lh; when the text width (twice the text length times `CU_TW` times the height) is more than 0.9 of the box width, the height is lowered so that the text width is exactly 0.9 of the box width, and then the height is at most 0.8 of the box height. Its rotation is 0. Its anchor x is the item's lx minus half the text width, then kept from the box's smallest x up to its largest x minus the text width; its anchor y is ly plus 0.35 times the height, then kept from the box's smallest y plus the height up to its largest y.

`cu_base(k)` builds the cut geometry of piece k unmirrored, moved so its cutting loop starts at 0,0, as a new item, and returns the item's index. It calls `cu_prepare(k)`, takes the bounding box of `CULP` with `cu_bbox` and moves everything by minus its smallest x and y, called sx and sy. The item's first is the current length of `CUR`. It then adds, in this order: the cutting loop as a `PK_LOOP` primitive on `LY_CUT` from the `CULP` points minus sx and sy (the item's cut is that primitive's index); a `pr_circ` on `LY_CUT` for every hole of `CUHQ` and `CUHR`, with the centre minus sx and sy; when `CU.seam` is true the stitching loop as a `PK_LOOP` on `LY_SCORE` from the `CUSP` points minus sx and sy; and when the piece's grainline ends g0 and g1 are more than 1 apart, `cu_grain` with ten times each end minus sx and sy. The item's n is the number of primitives added, piece is k, copy 0, copies 1, w and h are the box's width and height, area is the absolute value of `cu_sarea` of `CULP`, grain tells whether the grainline exists, the label length being the piece name's length plus 4 when the piece's cut is above one (room for the copy count like " 1/2") and the name's length otherwise, lh is the smaller of w and h times 0.09 limited to between 7 and 20 and then lowered, when the label length times twice `CU_TW` times lh would be more than 0.7 of w, to that limit but never below 4, ox and oy are 0, tr and tc are 0, tiled is false, sheet is minus one, rot 0, px and py 0. The label centre lx and ly is the average of the points of the stitching outline (`CUSP` when `CU.seam` is true, else `CULP`) minus sx and sy; when there is a grainline whose ends differ at least as much in y as in x and the absolute difference between lx and the grainline's middle x (minus sx) is below half a text width (the label length times `CU_TW` times lh, which already is half the text width) plus lh, the label moves beside the grainline: with gx the grainline's middle x and xl and xr the x of the nearest crossings, left and right of gx, of the horizontal line at height ly with the stitching outline (`CUSP` when `CU.seam` is true, else `CULP`, minus sx and sy; xl is 0 and xr is w when there is no crossing on that side), the right space is xr minus gx minus lh and the left space is gx minus xl minus lh, the right side is chosen when its space is at least the left one's, space is the larger of the two, and when twice the half text width is more than space, lh becomes the larger of 4 and lh times space divided by twice the half text width (and is the lh the item gets); then, with the half text width worked out again from that lh, lx becomes gx plus the half text width plus lh on the right side and gx minus the half text width minus lh on the left side. When instead the grainline is more horizontal than vertical (the absolute x difference of its ends above their absolute y difference) and the absolute difference between ly and the grainline's middle y (minus sy) is below lh, ly becomes that middle y minus lh when that is at least 1.5 times lh, and that middle y plus lh otherwise.

`cu_clone(src, mirror, copy, copies)` makes a new item from the item src and returns its index. The new item is the same as src except for copy, copies, first (the current length of `CUR`), n and cut. Every primitive of src (those from its first for its n that are not `PK_TEXT`) is recreated with `pr_new` and the same kind, layer and r, with its points copied (each read by index into a local first), x replaced by src's w minus x when mirror is true. The new cut is the index of the copy of src's cut primitive. When mirror is true the new lx is w minus lx. Then `cu_label` is called for the new item with an empty tile text, and its n is set to the number of primitives added, including the label.

`cu_cols(w)` is the number of skyline columns of a sheet w millimetres wide: w divided by `CU_CW`, rounded down, plus one.

`cu_dims()` returns the width and the height in millimetres of the chosen sheet: `CU.cw` and `CU.ch` when `CU.sheet` is 12 (custom), each never below 20, otherwise the entries of `CU_PW` and `CU_PH` at `CU.sheet`; a height of zero means a roll of any length.

`cu_ov(uw, uh)` is the overlap between neighbouring tiles: `CU_OVER`, limited to a quarter of the smaller of uw and uh, where an uh of zero or less is ignored and only uw counts.

`cu_fits(i, uw, uh)` tells whether item i can lie inside a region uw wide and uh high, an uh of zero or less meaning unlimited height: it is true when the item's w is at most uw and (uh is zero or less, or its h is at most uh), or when turning is allowed (`CU.grain` is false or the item has no grain) and its h is at most uw and (uh is zero or less, or its w is at most uh).

`cu_clip_loop(first, n, x0, y0, x1, y1)` clips the closed polygon of the n points of `CUP` from index first to the rectangle from x0, y0 to x1, y1 by Sutherland-Hodgman, and leaves the result in `CUC1`. It copies the points into `CUC1`, then for each of the four sides in the order x at least x0, x at most x1, y at least y0, y at most y1 it builds the clipped polygon in `CUC2` and copies it back into `CUC1`. For each edge of the current polygon from cur to the next point nxt, a point is inside when it is on the kept side of the line (on the line counts as inside): both inside pushes nxt; cur inside and nxt outside pushes the crossing point; cur outside and nxt inside pushes the crossing point and then nxt; both outside pushes nothing. The crossing point is `plerp` from cur to nxt at the parameter where the clipped coordinate reaches the side's value.

`cu_clip_path(first, n, closed, layer, x0, y0, x1, y1)` clips the polyline of the n points of `CUP` from index first, plus the closing edge from the last point back to the first when closed is true, to the rectangle from x0, y0 to x1, y1, and appends the visible parts as `PK_PATH` primitives on the layer with every point moved by minus x0 and minus y0. Each edge is clipped with the Liang-Barsky method over the parameter range 0 to 1, dropped when nothing of it is inside, and touching the rectangle counts as inside. A kept part continues the primitive being built when its start is within 0.001 of that primitive's last point, so only its end is pushed; otherwise a new primitive is started with `pr_new` and both its start and end are pushed. Points of `CUP` are read by index into locals before anything is pushed.

`cu_tile_id(out, r, c)` appends the name of the tile in row r and column c: the letter that is r places after 'A', then c plus one as a number.

`cu_cross(at, text)` draws a registration mark on `LY_SCORE`: two `pr_line` lines, one horizontal and one vertical, each 7 long and crossing at the point at, and a `pr_text` label of height 3.5 with rotation 0 and the given text, anchored at the point at moved 4 along x and minus 2 along y.

`cu_regs(src, r, c, rows, cols, sx, sy, ov, x0, y0, x1, y1)` adds registration marks to the tile whose primitives are being appended right now. src is the item that is being split, and its cut loop (the primitive `CUR` at its cut) says where there is material. The tile is in row r and column c of rows by cols, with steps sx and sy, overlap ov and covers x0 to x1 and y0 to y1 of the source. For each neighbour that exists the shared overlap strip has a centre line: for the right neighbour (c plus one below cols) x is (c plus one) times sx plus ov over two; for the left neighbour (c above 0) x is c times sx plus ov over two; for the neighbour below (r plus one below rows) y is (r plus one) times sy plus ov over two; for the one above (r above 0) y is r times sy plus ov over two. Along the other axis the candidates are the points at 0.25, 0.5 and 0.75 of the tile's own range (x0 to x1, or y0 to y1). Every candidate point that is inside the source cut loop by `cu_inside` (on the points of `CUP` from the cut primitive's first for its n) gets a `cu_cross` at that point minus x0 and y0, with the text made of two tile names from `cu_tile_id` joined by a slash, the earlier tile first: right pairs (r, c) with (r, c plus one), left pairs (r, c minus one) with (r, c), below pairs (r, c) with (r plus one, c), above pairs (r minus one, c) with (r, c). When `CU.layers` is false it adds nothing, because registration marks are not cut.

`cu_tile(src, r, c, rows, cols, uw, uh)` cuts the tile in row r and column c out of the item src and appends it to `CUI` as a new item, returning true, or returns false when nothing of the piece falls inside the tile. With ov from `cu_ov`, sx is uw minus ov and sy is uh minus ov when uh is above zero. The tile covers x from x0, c times sx, to x1, the smaller of x0 plus uw and src's w; and y from y0, r times sy, to y1, the smaller of y0 plus uh and src's h, or, when uh is zero or less, from 0 to src's h. The cut loop of src is clipped to that rectangle with `cu_clip_loop`; its box and area (absolute `cu_sarea`) are taken from `CUC1` straight after, and with fewer than 3 points or an area below 1 the function returns false. The new item's first is the current length of `CUR`. It gets the clipped loop as a `PK_LOOP` on `LY_CUT` with points moved by minus x0 and y0 (that is its cut), then for each other primitive of src that is not text: a `PK_CIRC` on `LY_CUT` whose whole circle lies inside the rectangle is recreated with `pr_circ` moved the same way; a `PK_LOOP` on another layer is clipped with `cu_clip_path` as a closed path; a `PK_PATH` is clipped with `cu_clip_path` as an open path; then `cu_regs`. The new item is src with first, n (the number of primitives it has, including its label), cut, tr set to r, tc to c, tiled true, ox x0, oy y0, w and h from the clipped loop's box, area from it, sheet minus one, and the label centre lx and ly the middle of that box; its label comes from `cu_label` with the text of `cu_tile_id` for r and c.

`cu_split(src, uw, uh)` replaces item src by tiles that fit a region uw by uh (an uh of zero or less means unlimited height). With ov from `cu_ov`, the column count is the smaller of 40 and the larger of 1 and the rounded up (w minus ov) divided by (uw minus ov), and the row count is 1 when uh is zero or less, otherwise computed the same way from h and uh. It calls `cu_tile` for every row and, within a row, every column, in order. Finally it sets the sheet of src to minus two so nesting skips it.

`cu_rotbox(i, rot)` returns the smallest x, smallest y, largest x and largest y of the cut loop of item i (the points of `CUP` for the primitive `CUR` at its cut) after each point is turned by `cu_rot` with rot.

`cu_profile(i, rot)` computes the column profile of item i turned by rot quarter turns and returns the turned width and height. The turned polygon is the cut loop of the item with every point turned by `cu_rot` and moved so that its smallest x and y (from `cu_rotbox`) become 0. `CU_DG` becomes `CU.gap` divided by `CU_CW`, rounded up. The raw column count is the turned width divided by `CU_CW`, rounded down, plus one. `CURT` (starting at 1e18) and `CURB` (starting at minus 1e18) get the raw column count plus twice `CU_DG` entries, entry j standing for the x range starting at (j minus `CU_DG`) times `CU_CW`. For every edge from point a to point b of the turned polygon, including the closing edge, with lo and hi the smaller and larger x, and for every column jj from lo divided by `CU_CW` rounded down (at least 0) up to hi divided by `CU_CW` rounded down (at most the raw count minus one): xa is the larger of lo and jj times `CU_CW`, xb is the smaller of hi and (jj plus one) times `CU_CW`, ya and yb are the y of the edge at xa and at xb (when hi minus lo is below 1e-9 they are the y of a and the y of b); the entry of `CURT` at jj plus `CU_DG` becomes the smaller of itself, ya and yb, and the entry of `CURB` there the larger. Finally `CUTOP` and `CUBOT` get the same count of entries, where `CUTOP` at j is the smallest `CURT` and `CUBOT` at j the largest `CURB` among the entries from j minus `CU_DG` to j plus `CU_DG` that exist.

`cu_find(i, s, rot)` looks for the best position of item i on sheet s turned by rot, given the skyline of the sheet. It uses the width wr and height hr that `cu_profile` returns for it, the sheet's w and h (zero h meaning unlimited) and the margin m of `CU.margin`. It returns four values: whether a position exists, the bottom edge of the piece there, the column step xi and the top y. No position exists when wr is more than w minus twice m plus 0.001, or h is above zero and hr is more than h minus twice m plus 0.001, or there is no step. Steps run from xi0, m divided by `CU_CW` minus 1e-9 rounded up, to xi1, (w minus m minus wr) divided by `CU_CW` plus 1e-9 rounded down. For each step xi the y starts at m; for every profile column j whose `CUTOP` is below 1e17 the sheet column c is xi plus j minus `CU_DG`, and when c is from 0 to `cu_cols` of w minus one, the skyline value `CUK` at the sheet's sky offset plus c, minus `CUTOP` at j, is a lower bound for y; y is the largest of m and these bounds. The bottom is y plus hr. A step is skipped when h is above zero and bottom is above h minus m plus 0.001. The best step has the smallest bottom; when bottoms differ by less than 0.0001 the smaller y wins, then the smaller xi.

`cu_try(i, s)` tries every allowed turn of item i on sheet s with `cu_find`: only 0 and 2 when `CU.grain` is true and the item has a grain, otherwise 0, 1, 2 and 3. It returns whether any worked, the best rot, xi and y, the best being the one with the smallest bottom, the earlier rot winning ties within 0.0001.

`cu_place(i, s, rot, xi, y)` puts item i on sheet s turned by rot with its top left at x equal to xi times `CU_CW` and the given y. It calls `cu_profile(i, rot)` and `cu_rotbox(i, rot)`, then raises the skyline: for every profile column j with `CUBOT` above minus 1e17 and sheet column c equal to xi plus j minus `CU_DG` from 0 to `cu_cols` of the sheet's w minus one, the skyline `CUK` at the sheet's sky offset plus c becomes the larger of itself and y plus `CUBOT` at j plus `CU.gap`. The sheet's used becomes the larger of itself and y plus the turned height, and its count goes up by one. Every primitive of the item (from its first for its n) is recreated with `pr_new` with the same kind, layer and r, its sheet set to s, its tx and tn copied and its rot set to the old rot plus 90 times the turn; each point is turned by `cu_rot`, moved by minus the box's smallest x and y, and moved by the position x and y. The item's sheet, rot, px and py are set.

`cu_sheet_new()` appends a `CuSheet` from `cu_dims()` with that w and h, roll true when h is zero or less, used 0, sky the current length of `CUK` and count 0, pushes `cu_cols` of w copies of `CU.margin` onto `CUK`, and returns the new sheet's index.

`cu_nest()` clears `CUS` and `CUK`, collects the indexes of the items of `CUI` whose sheet is minus one, and sorts them by w times h, largest first, ties by smaller index first. For each, in that order, it tries each existing sheet in order with `cu_try` and places the item with `cu_place` on the first that works; when none works it makes a `cu_sheet_new` sheet and tries that one; when even that fails the item's sheet is set to minus three. Last, each roll sheet gets its h set to its used plus `CU.margin`, rounded up to a whole number.

`cu_build()` rebuilds everything from the current pieces. It clears `CUP`, `CUB`, `CUR`, `CUI`, `CUS` and `CUK` and returns when there are no `PIECES`. It takes the sheet's width and height from `cu_dims()`, and uw is the width minus twice the margin minus `CU_CW` (room for the rounding of positions to whole columns) and uh is the height minus twice the margin when the height is above zero and 0 otherwise, each of them at least 20 when above zero. For each piece k it makes the base item with `cu_base(k)`, then for each copy c below the larger of the piece's cut and 1 it makes `cu_clone` of the base with mirror true when the piece is not folded and c is odd, copy c and that many copies; if the clone does not fit (`cu_fits` with uw and uh) it is replaced by tiles with `cu_split`. The base item's sheet is set to minus two. Last it calls `cu_nest()`.

`cu_on(layer)` is true for layer `LY_CUT` always, and for the other layers only when `CU.layers` is true.

`cu_body_svg(out, s)` appends the SVG layer groups of sheet s. For each layer 0, 1 and 2 for which `cu_on` is true (named CUT, SCORE and ENGRAVE, with the colours #ff0000, #0000ff and #000000) it writes `<g id="NAME" inkscape:groupmode="layer" inkscape:label="NAME" fill="none" stroke="COLOUR" stroke-width="0.1">` and a newline, then one line for each primitive of `CUR` on that layer whose sheet is s, then `</g>` and a newline. A `PK_LOOP` is `<path d="M x y L x y L x y Z"/>` with its points; a `PK_PATH` is the same path without the Z; a `PK_CIRC` is `<circle cx="x" cy="y" r="r"/>`; a `PK_TEXT` is `<text x="x" y="y" font-size="h" font-family="sans-serif"` followed, when its rot is not zero, by ` transform="rotate(rot x y)"`, then `>`, the characters of the text taken from `CUB` at its tx for its tn and escaped with `esc`, and `</text>`. Every number is written with `cu_num` and 2 decimals.

`cu_svg(out, s)` writes sheet s as a complete SVG file: the line `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>`, then the root `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:inkscape="http://www.inkscape.org/namespaces/inkscape" width="Wmm" height="Hmm" viewBox="0 0 W H">` and a newline, `cu_body_svg`, and `</svg>` with a newline, where W and H are the sheet's w and h written with 2 decimals.

`cu_preview(out, s)` writes the on-screen SVG of sheet s: the root `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:inkscape="http://www.inkscape.org/namespaces/inkscape" class="cutsvg" role="img" aria-label="Nesting preview" viewBox="0 0 W H">`, then `<rect class="cutsheet" x="0" y="0" width="W" height="H"/>`, then `cu_body_svg`, then `</svg>`, with W and H the sheet's w and h to 2 decimals.

`dxs(out, code, s)` appends the group code number, a newline, the text s and a newline. `dxi(out, code, v)` does the same with the whole number v, and `dxr(out, code, v)` with the real v written by `cu_num` with 3 decimals.

`cu_dxf(out, s)` writes sheet s as a DXF R12 ASCII file with `dxs`, `dxi` and `dxr`, in millimetres with y pointing up, so every y written is the sheet's h minus the stored y. HEADER section: `0 SECTION`, `2 HEADER`, `9 $ACADVER` then `1 AC1009`, `9 $INSUNITS` then `70 4`, `9 $MEASUREMENT` then `70 1`, `9 $EXTMIN` then `10 0.0` and `20 0.0`, `9 $EXTMAX` then `10` the sheet's w and `20` its h, then `0 ENDSEC`. TABLES section: `0 SECTION`, `2 TABLES`; the LTYPE table is `0 TABLE`, `2 LTYPE`, `70 1`, `0 LTYPE`, `2 CONTINUOUS`, `70 0`, `3 Solid line`, `72 65`, `73 0`, `40 0.0`, `0 ENDTAB`; the LAYER table is `0 TABLE`, `2 LAYER`, `70` and the number of layers in use (1 or 3, by `cu_on`), then for each layer in use (CUT with colour 1, SCORE with colour 5, ENGRAVE with colour 7) `0 LAYER`, `2` and its name, `70 0`, `62` and its colour, `6 CONTINUOUS`, then `0 ENDTAB`; the STYLE table is `0 TABLE`, `2 STYLE`, `70 1`, `0 STYLE`, `2 STANDARD`, `70 0`, `40 0.0`, `41 1.0`, `50 0.0`, `71 0`, `42 2.5`, `3 txt`, `0 ENDTAB`; then `0 ENDSEC`. BLOCKS section: `0 SECTION`, `2 BLOCKS`, `0 ENDSEC`. ENTITIES section: `0 SECTION`, `2 ENTITIES`, then for each layer in use and each primitive of `CUR` on that layer whose sheet is s: a `PK_CIRC` is `0 CIRCLE`, `8` layer name, `10` x, `20` y, `30 0.0`, `40` radius; a `PK_PATH` with exactly two points is `0 LINE`, `8` layer name, `10` x1, `20` y1, `30 0.0`, `11` x2, `21` y2, `31 0.0`; a `PK_LOOP`, or a `PK_PATH` with another number of points, is `0 POLYLINE`, `8` layer name, `66 1`, `10 0.0`, `20 0.0`, `30 0.0`, `70` with 1 for a loop and 0 for a path, then for each point `0 VERTEX`, `8` layer name, `10` x, `20` y, `30 0.0`, and finally `0 SEQEND` and `8` layer name; a `PK_TEXT` is `0 TEXT`, `8` layer name, `10` x, `20` y, `30 0.0`, `40` height, `1` the text with every byte outside printable ASCII replaced by a question mark, `50` the angle, which is minus rot brought into the range from 0 up to 360, and `7 STANDARD`. After the section comes `0 ENDSEC` and `0 EOF`.

`cu_plt(out, s)` writes sheet s as HPGL with 40 plotter units per millimetre: unit(v) is v times 40 rounded to the nearest whole number, and a point is written as unit(x) and unit of the sheet's h minus y, whole numbers separated by a comma. It writes `IN;` and a newline, then for each layer in use (`cu_on`, with pen 1 for CUT, pen 2 for SCORE and pen 3 for ENGRAVE) `SP`, the pen number, `;` and a newline, followed by the primitives of that layer on sheet s. A path or a loop is `PU` and its first point, `;PD` and the remaining points separated by commas, `;` and a newline; a loop repeats its first point at the end. A circle is drawn as a closed path of 24 straight segments through 25 points (angle 0 to 360 degrees in 24 equal steps starting on the positive x side) at centre plus radius times cosine and sine. A text is `PU` and its point, `;SI` w`,`h `;DI` c`,`s `;LB` the text, a byte with value 3, `;` and a newline, where h is the text height divided by 10 and w is 0.7 times h, both with 3 decimals, and c and s are the cosine and sine of minus rot (rot is in degrees) with 3 decimals. After all layers it writes `PU;` and a newline, then `SP0;` and a newline.

`cu_ext()` is the file extension for `CU.fmt`: "dxf" for 0, "svg" for 1 and "plt" for 2.

`cu_mime()` is the media type for `CU.fmt`: "image/vnd.dxf" for 0, "image/svg+xml" for 1 and "application/vnd.hp-HPGL" for 2.

`cu_file(out, s)` writes sheet s in the format `CU.fmt`: 0 `cu_dxf`, 1 `cu_svg`, 2 `cu_plt`.

`cu_filename(out, stem, s)` appends the stem, then "-cut-sheet-", s plus one, "-of-", the number of sheets in `CUS`, a dot and `cu_ext()`.

`cu_info(out)` appends a summary such as "3 sheets · 61% of the material used · 14 parts": the number of sheets (with "sheet" in the singular when there is one), the percentage, rounded to a whole number, of the summed areas of the placed items (those whose sheet is zero or more) over the summed w times h of the sheets, and the number of placed items. When some placed items are tiled it appends ", N of them tiles of oversize pieces" with N their count, and when some items have sheet minus three it appends " · M did not fit". With no sheets it appends "Nothing to cut".
